# CLAUDE.md — this project runs on the ACAF method

This project's memory lives in the `acaf/` folder, not in any conversation. The skills in `.claude/skills/` (acaf-onboard, acaf-plan, acaf-explore, acaf-build, acaf-review, acaf-accept, acaf-pickup, acaf-continue) are the workflow. These standing rules apply to every turn, whether or not a skill is invoked.

## Session start

Your first action in a fresh session (or after a context compaction) is the cross-session pickup: read `acaf/initiative_manifest.md`, `acaf/handoff_summary.md`, and the tails of `acaf/change_log.md` + `acaf/deviation_log.md`, then state in one short paragraph where things stand and what the next step is (the `/acaf-pickup` skill is this ritual, packaged). Do not start work from memory of a previous conversation — the files are the truth.

## Which skill, when (routing)

When a message does not name a skill, route it yourself by intent — do not do method-covered work "bare":

| The user's message means… | Use |
|---|---|
| A fresh session, "where were we", "status" | `acaf-pickup` |
| The project has no `acaf/` folder yet | `acaf-onboard` (before anything else) |
| Start something new/non-trivial ("add X", "build Y", "fix Z") with NO active initiative | `acaf-plan` — never build unplanned work |
| A challenge with no proven approach, an approach failing its goal or getting worse, or "what are our options?" | `acaf-explore` — the option study: every option regardless of cost, each one's levers, a trial of each, a cost and trade-off report; the user chooses, then `acaf-plan` |
| "continue", "next", "keep going", or "what now / not sure what's next" | `acaf-continue` — reads the state, then advances one step (build / review / accept) or proposes directions if nothing's in flight |
| A direct build ask while an initiative is active ("build the next slice") | `acaf-build` (one slice) — `acaf-continue` routes here too when slices remain |
| "review this", "check the work", "get a second opinion" | `acaf-review` |
| "are we done?", "close it", "accept", "wrap up" | `acaf-accept` |
| "park this: …" | Append an `FB-<n>` line to `acaf/follow_up_backlog.md` — no skill, no derail |

Escape hatch: a genuinely trivial mechanical fix (a typo, a broken import) may skip the full plan/build ceremony — but it still gets a `change_log.md` row and its own commit, and if it turns out non-trivial mid-edit, stop and route through `acaf-plan`. When unsure which skill applies, `acaf-pickup` first — the records answer it.

## The working discipline

- **One initiative at a time.** New requests during an active initiative are either a bounded detour that returns, or a parked `FB-<n>` entry in `acaf/follow_up_backlog.md`. Don't silently swap the work in flight.
- **Plan before building.** Non-trivial work gets an initiative via `/acaf-plan`: acceptance criteria upfront (present tense), a decision record with alternatives, a slice plan whose definitions of done list source files first, then tests, then records.
- **Explore before committing to an approach.** A new challenge — or an approach that keeps failing or getting worse — gets an option study (`/acaf-explore`): every option regardless of cost, each option's levers turned before it is dropped, every option tried and reported with its cost. Never dismiss an option in your head, and never keep polishing an approach whose ceiling is the problem.
- **Build in slices.** One slice = implement + gates + records + ONE commit (`/acaf-build`). Declare the files you will change before changing them; adding one undeclared file is fine if you say so; needing many means re-plan. The initiative's `build-mode` (chosen at plan time) sets the cadence: `one-at-a-time` stops after each slice so you see the diff; `all-at-once` builds the slices back-to-back — still one gated commit each — and halts on any failure. Never two slices in one commit.
- **Don't park the obvious.** A small, obvious fix found mid-slice gets done right after the slice lands, as its own recorded change (a `change_log.md` row and its own commit). `FB-<n>` entries are for the user's own ideas and for genuinely non-trivial discoveries that need a plan.
- **The no-silent-success rule.** After any change-making step, verify the declared files exist AND actually changed, and that the gates ran green. A step that changed nothing, or whose gates are red, is reported as a failure in plain words — not softened, not buried in an otherwise-green report.
- **Record as you go.** Every change → a `change_log.md` row. Every divergence from a plan → a `deviation_log.md` `DEV-<n>` entry with the why. Every non-trivial choice → a decision record. If a past record turns out wrong, correct it visibly; don't rewrite history silently.
- **The contract vs the index.** `functional_contract.md` holds verified promises (what MUST be true — criteria graduate into it only at acceptance). `functionality_index.md` holds the as-built truth (what IS — updated whenever capability changes, always by reading the real code). Keep them distinct, and neither should claim more than the code contains.
- **Verification uses the project's real commands** (see the `<!-- verification -->` marker in the contract). Never invent a command the project cannot run.
- **Human checks are batched.** Things only the user can judge collect into the acceptance checklist (`/acaf-accept`); do not interrupt builds for them.

## Communication

- Plain, specific words. Complete sentences. Name real files (as clickable paths) and real numbers ("tests 42 → 45") instead of adjectives.
- End each work turn with a short status: what was done (with verdicts), anything parked or deviated, and a **What's next** block — the recommended action first, in your own voice.
- **Do the work you own; don't hand it back as a command to type.** If the next step is something you perform — running the independent reviewer, the mechanical part of acceptance, refreshing a record — run it, or offer it in one plain sentence (`Say "review it" → I run the independent reviewer`). Slash-commands exist for when the user chooses to steer; your recommendations are actions in your own voice, not the user's homework.
- **When the real open question is which DIRECTION to take next, you do the ideation — never ask a bare "what now?".** You hold the whole record set: `follow_up_backlog.md`, the gaps and unmet rows in `functional_contract.md`, principles in `vision_principles.md` not yet realized, and any PRD/intent docs. Read them and bring 2–4 concrete candidate directions, each with a one-line why, its source, and the exact words to pick it (`Say "canvas parity" → I plan the gold-standard zoom/pan work (FB-2)`). Recommend the strongest, but make them real choices — never make the user supply an idea you could have drawn from the files.
- Curated, not exhaustive: the 2–4 you genuinely think are best, not every path that exists. *(That is for direction picks. Solving a challenge is the opposite: exhaustive in the exploration — `acaf-explore` — and curated only in the recommendation.)* For a decision that is NOT a direction pick, ask ONE question, name the trade-off, and lead with your recommendation.

## Git

- One commit per slice, plain-language message, first line under ~70 characters. Commit only what the slice touched plus its records. Never push unless asked.
- **Verify the repo-local identity before the FIRST commit (and after any clone/init).** On a machine with multiple git accounts, the global identity may belong to a different account than this project — `git config user.email` must match the account this project belongs to, set repo-locally. If this file has a "Machine note" section below, it is authoritative for this machine; follow it exactly.
